ommands and compound commands can be denied independently of an allowed CLI: a helper denial does not mean all Bash commands are unavailable. Do not repeat a denied command or bypass its restriction; use only the separately authorized CLI commands. If the CLI itself is missing or denied, use a configured native safe-output function only if it is explicitly exposed in your tool inventory. Report unavailable functionality with `report_missing_tool` when configured and reachable; reserve `report_incomplete` for failed write attempts. If no supported transport is available, stop and report the limitation in the final response; do not claim the action was emitted or use authenticated GitHub commands as a substitute.\n\nPass the complete JSON object inline as one single-quoted shell argument. Inside JSON strings, encode newlines as `\\n`, double quotes as `\\\"`, backslashes as `\\\\`, and apostrophes as `\\u0027` so they do not end the shell quote. Unicode can remain literal. The binary comes first, so a malformed payload still runs the CLI and reports an error:\n```bash\nsafeoutputs add_comment '{\"item_number\":42,\"body\":\"### Result\\n\\nIt\\u0027s ready.\\nQuoted text: \\\"hello\\\".\\nLiteral path: C:\\\\temp\\\\file.txt\"}'\n```\nThis preserves multiline content without auxiliary tools, pipelines, temporary files, or shell expansion. Adapt fields to the enabled tool's schema. Raw Markdown and `@filename` references are not JSON payloads.\n\nStdin is also supported: `safeoutputs <tool_name> .` reads a complete JSON object from stdin — the entire stdin content must itself be valid JSON; never pipe a raw file or raw Markdown into this bare form. To pass one field's raw text verbatim with no JSON escaping, target that single field instead: `cat file.md | safeoutputs <tool_name> --<field> .` substitutes the field's value with the raw stdin text. Never write a bare `.` as a value inside a quoted JSON literal (e.g. `{\"body\": .}`) — that is not valid JSON. CLI proxying